<h3 id="stacked-tables-resolution">The resolution is a design decision</h3>
<p>
  Notice what kind of problem this is. There is no clever CSS that makes "two stacked,
  unbounded tables" stable, because the instability is in the <em>concept</em>: two
  regions of unknowable size competing for one axis. The fixes all involve changing the
  design, which is exactly why design has to be in the room:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <strong>Partition the viewport deliberately.</strong> Give each table a fixed share of
    the content area (not necessarily 50/50: weighted by expected volume) and let each
    scroll <em>internally</em>. The frame never moves; only content inside each pane
    changes. This is the strongest containment, and it is the running worked example of
    this guide (<a href="16-worked-example.html">§16</a>).
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Cap the secondary table.</strong> Decide one table is primary and give the
    other a fixed budget: "5 most recent submissions" plus a "View all" link to its own
    page. Fixed row count, fixed height, fully containable; and often better UX than the
    original.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Don't stack them.</strong> Tabs, a master-detail split, or side-by-side panes
    each remove the shared vertical axis entirely. If the user rarely needs both tables at
    once, the stack was the wrong composition to begin with.
  </li>
</ul>
