<h2 id="seed-data">11.5 Seed data is part of the discipline</h2>
<p>
  There is a development-side habit that keeps all of the above honest:
  <strong>test with data volumes that vary wildly.</strong>
  <Chip k="very" /> A dev database seeded with three tidy
  rows per table is the engineering version of the happy-path comp: every layout looks
  contained because nothing ever has enough content to fight. The failure modes in this
  chapter only become visible when the seed data forces them: one table with 1 row above
  a table with 50; a program title that wraps to three lines; a list that is legitimately
  empty; a page where one region resolves instantly and its sibling takes two seconds.
</p>
<p>
  So seed generously and unevenly, on purpose: some entities with 0 children, some with
  1, some with 5, some with 50 or 500. Make the skew part of the fixture set rather than
  something you construct by hand once and lose. And when a layout survives the skewed
  seed <em>and</em> a throttled network, it has actually earned the word "stable":
  the states design signed off on and the states the seed data forces are, together, the
  whole state space.
</p>

<Callout kind="info" title="The checklist, for design reviews">
  <p>
    For every data-driven region in the mock: (1) What renders before the data?
    (2) What does 0 / 1 / 50 items look like? (3) When it's bigger than its space: grow,
    scroll, paginate, or truncate? (4) Which regions arrive at different times, and does
    the page look intentional in both orders? (5) Does anything already painted ever
    move? If a question has no answer, the mock isn't done; if the answer is "it moves,"
    the design isn't stable.
  </p>
</Callout>

<h2 id="rules">The rules</h2>
<table class="tbl">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Rule</th>
      <th>Scope</th>
      <th>Importance</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>
        <strong>Keep the engineering/UX channel open, in both directions:</strong>
        containment concerns surface while a design is still a design, not after the
        layout is built.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="ux" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="critical" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>
        Every data-driven region in a mock needs its <strong>pre-data, empty, sparse, and
        overflowing states</strong> answered before it is approved.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="ux" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="critical" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>
        <strong>Regions of unknown size must not compete for one axis.</strong> Partition
        the space, cap one region, or change the composition.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="ux" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="critical" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>
        Overlays and dialogs get <strong>one frame per flow</strong>, sized for the
        largest step; steps change inside the frame.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="ux" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="very" /></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>
        Seed data <strong>skewed on purpose</strong> (0 / 1 / 5 / 50 / 500) so unstable
        layouts fail in development, not in front of users.
      </td>
      <td><Chip k="ux" /></td>
      <td><Chip k="very" /></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

